\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\title{From reflection generation to Pin and Spin}

\p{One reflection can enlarge the fixed subspace of an orthogonal
transformation. Iterating this correction proves reflection generation and
makes the unnormalized Lipschitz action onto the orthogonal group. Passing to
the norm-one Pin and Spin carriers requires scalar normalization: determinant
parity controls evenness, while the spinor norm measures the remaining
obstruction over a general field.}

\p{Extending the field preserves the spinor norm through the map on square
classes. A quadratic extension can make a nontrivial obstruction vanish;
the resulting pair of Spin lifts records this change through its Galois action.}

\p{Isotropic transvections give a complementary explicit family in the Spin
image: their nilpotent Clifford lifts add through the quotient
#{u^{\perp}/Ku}. A null-frame calculation exhibits the quadratic action term,
while a separate factorization retains a radical PGA shear inside the
vector-generated carrier.}

\p{Over a local field, \ref{fcap-0044} gives the Clifford and matrix
carriers their canonical topologies through finite coordinates. The resulting closed carriers
and continuous actions are established in \ref{fcap-0045}. The open
Clifford quotient in \ref{fcap-0046} makes the spinor obstruction locally
constant. Properness of the Spin projection then identifies compactness
with anisotropy in \ref{fcap-0047}; the two binary forms over
#{\mathbb Q_3} in \ref{fcap-0048} exhibit the compact and split cases.
The images of the local spinor norms are computed in \ref{fcap-0049}
and \ref{fcap-004A}: binary norm subgroups and higher-rank surjectivity
give the precise indices of the Spin images in the special orthogonal groups.
The worked example \ref{fcap-004B} follows a rational spinor obstruction
that vanishes over #{\mathbb Q_7} and constructs the resulting Spin lift.
The real topology gives the compact Spin covers below.}

\p{To treat a rational transformation at all finite primes at once,
\ref{fcap-004C} specifies compatible compact-open reference subgroups
for its orthogonal and Spin actions. The rational-basis construction in
\ref{fcap-004D} supplies such subgroups: each rational point belongs
at all but finitely many primes, with the exceptions determined by its
own matrix denominators.}

\p{Changing the rational basis or shrinking the reference subgroups
at finitely many primes preserves this eventual membership by
\ref{fcap-004E}. The \vocabk{restricted product}{fcap-004F} then
collects all local transformations satisfying it into a locally
compact group. Its finite stages allow finitely many unrestricted
components while retaining compact-open conditions elsewhere.}

\p{Deleting any set of prime coordinates gives the
\vocabk{away restricted product}{fcap-004G}; a finite deleted set
splits off as unrestricted local factors. Adjoining the unrestricted
real factor then gives the \ref{fcap-004H} full adelic groups.
Their product topology distinguishes a compact finite-prime reference
subgroup from an open neighborhood in the full group.}

\p{A prime that keeps moving gives coordinatewise convergence
without restricted-product convergence in \ref{fcap-004I}.
The continuous component maps in \ref{fcap-004J} connect Spin, SO
and O across the finite, away and full groups. Their Spin projection
kernels admit every family of local scalar signs in positive dimension.}

\p{A rational transformation determines compatible local points at
all completions through the \vocabk{rational diagonal}{fcap-004K}.
One retained completion already detects it. Under eventual lattice
containment, finite-place integrality and a small real neighborhood
isolate the identity in the full rational diagonal, as proved in
\ref{fcap-004L}.}

\p{At finite places alone, the factorial transvections of
\ref{fcap-004M} converge to the identity in one fixed stage,
so an isotropic rational SO diagonal in dimension at least three
is not discrete. The \vocabk{finite adelic spinor norm}{fcap-004N}
then collects local square-class obstructions in a restricted product
whose reference groups are the actual images of the chosen SO references.}

\p{The \vocabk{adelic spinor kernel}{fcap-004O} consists of the
restricted SO tuples whose local norms are all trivial. The Spin
projection factors continuously through it, and rational spinor
norms localize coordinatewise. The
\vocabk{finite adelic class sets}{fcap-004P} then identify tuples
under one rational left transformation and a right transformation
in the actual product of the chosen references, separately for
O, SO and Spin.}

